  {
    "pkattachmentId": 71036,
    "bookid": 84,
    "chapterId": 104,
    "fileId": 48491,
    "type": "WSI",
    "diagnosis": "<p>Benign phyllodes tumours with squamoid nests</p>",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "<p>Benign phyllodes tumour shows elongated epithelium-lined clefts with areas of mild stromal hypercellularity.&nbsp; The borders of circumscribed and pushing, without any stromal overgrowth, significant stromal atypia or mitotic activity.&nbsp; Focally, sclerotic portions of the stroma show small epithelial nests that have a squamoid appearance.&nbsp;</p>",
    "source": "Imported from 5th edition<br><br><p>SGH Pathology</p>",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/7659.dzi",
    "thumbnail": null
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 71037,
    "bookid": 84,
    "chapterId": 104,
    "fileId": 48492,
    "type": "WSI",
    "diagnosis": "<p>Malignant phyllodes tumours with liposarcoma</p>",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "<p>Malignant phyllodes tumour shows broad stromal fronds, hypercellularity, moderate to marked atypia and mitotic activity. Adipocytes are present within the tumour, which in areas display hyperchromatic scalloped nuclei in keeping with lipoblasts of liposarcoma. Presence of liposarcoma alone is not currently regarded as being diagnostic of a malignant grade in phyllodes tumour. In this case, malignancy is concluded based on a constellation of histological parameters.</p>",
    "source": "Imported from 5th edition<br><br><p>SGH Pathology (case contributed by Dr Mihir Gudi)</p>",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/7660.dzi",
    "thumbnail": null
